  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge has settled over the area and should stick \naround for the next several days, keeping things warm and dry for \nthe foreseeable future. A weak cold front is expected to move \nacross Colorado this afternoon, bringing a few hours of drier air \n(RH<20%) and breezy conditions (15-20 kts with gusts of 25-30 kts)\nto the eastern plains. As such, elevated fire weather conditions \nare expected this afternoon and evening in the plains. Highs today\nwill be in the mid 60s-70s for the mountains and in the low 80s \nfor the plains and urban corridor. Tomorrow will be the coolest \nday of the week behind today's cold front, with highs expected to \nbe a few degrees cooler than today. \n\nTemperatures climb back up into the mid-80s on Monday, reaching near-\nrecord values for Denver, and then drop by a few degrees again on \nTuesday. A weak shortwave is still expected to push across the state \non Tuesday, so we've kept PoPs at 20-30% for scattered showers for \nmost of the higher terrain, with the highest chances for \nprecipitation concentrated over Park and Clear Creek Counties. After \nthe shortwave moves out of the area, the upper-level ridge should \nsettle back in, keeping Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday warm and dry \nwith highs in the mid 60s/70s for the mountains and low 80s for the \nplains.\n\nAs of the latest model consensus, the ridge pattern should hold \nthrough Saturday before moving eastward Saturday evening into Sunday \nmorning. That being said, there's still a good amount of uncertainty \nwith this, as some models have the ridge starting to move earlier, \nwhile some have it sticking around a little later into Sunday.",
